1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,810
An infinite proof
with dominoes

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:06,430
Dominoes lined up forever —
how can we be sure they all fall?

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,600
We only need to check two things:
(1) the first one falls  (2) if one falls, the next one falls

4
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:11,590
What if (2) fails? (a gap that's too wide)

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,860
It stops partway

6
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:16,230
What if there's no (1)? Nothing falls at all

7
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:19,360
If (1) and (2) are both true…

8
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:22,730
Every single one falls, forever!

9
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:26,200
Let's check this formula for every n,
just like dominoes

10
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:29,470
First domino: it's true for n = 1

11
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:33,040
If it's true up to n, adding n+1
gives exactly the formula for the next step

12
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:36,540
Check just two steps and it holds forever!
= mathematical induction

13
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:41,290
See you in the next episode!
